<h2 id="formatted-writing">Bring formatted writing from a document</h2>
<ol><li>Copy an article selection from Word, Google Docs, or a web page.</li><li>In the workspace or a converter, select <strong>Import formatted writing</strong>. Paste into the empty import box. If it already contains pending text, select all before replacing it.</li><li>Choose <strong>Preview selected Word file or pasted article</strong>. Inspect both the reading preview and the converted Markdown, including the listed omissions.</li><li>For a table without marked header cells, check <strong>Use the first table row as headers</strong> only if that row contains column names, then preview again.</li><li>Acknowledge that you reviewed the conversion, then choose <strong>Import into editor</strong>. Confirm replacement if you have an article already. Source notes are kept, previous valid writing is checkpointed, and export approval is cleared.</li></ol>
<p>Try <strong>Preview a formatted example</strong> in the import panel to see the flow with fictional writing. It stages an example for review without applying it. Normal pasting in the article editor still inserts plain text or Markdown.</p>
<h3>HTML files and clipboard differences</h3><p>A selected .html or .htm file is detected as HTML in automatic mode. You can also choose Formatted selection or HTML and paste HTML source. Files must be UTF-8 and at most 512 KiB. Raw HTML pasted in automatic Markdown mode remains literal Markdown; choose HTML deliberately to convert it. You can also choose a Word .docx file directly, as described below. Some source applications or clipboard permissions supply only plain text, which cannot recover lost document structure.</p>
<h3>HTML code or copied formatted writing?</h3><p>For code containing HTML tags, choose <strong>HTML source code</strong> in Import format, paste the code, then preview. That mode uses the plain text of your paste and ignores clipboard formatting. For a selection copied from a document or browser, choose <strong>Copied formatted writing</strong> and paste into the empty box; its preview opens immediately. You can use the dedicated <a href="/html-to-markdown/">HTML to Markdown converter</a> for code or HTML files. Preview notices and acknowledge the conversion before importing.</p>
<h3 id="html-relative-links">Keep relative HTML links</h3><p>For links such as <code>/about</code>, <code>../guide</code>, or <code>#details</code>, supply the original page address in <strong>Source page URL</strong> and preview again. Safe targets become absolute web links with a conversion notice. Fragment links point back to the original page. HTML base elements are ignored; only your explicit URL is used. No page or link is fetched. Without this URL, relative targets are omitted. Inspect the exact resulting Markdown before importing, especially query parameters. The temporary URL is excluded from backups as a setting and clears with applying/clearing the import, a new file, or workspace replacement. Resolved URLs become article text and are included in exports.</p>
<h3>What the conversion supports</h3><p>Common HTML headings, paragraphs, thematic breaks (&lt;hr&gt;), line breaks, bold and italic text, safe absolute web links, code, single-line nested lists, and simple tables with a header row. Common bold and italic span styles, combined emphasis, nested single-line emphasis, and formatting inside safe link labels are recognized. Adjacent runs with the same style are joined. Generated inline Markdown is checked against the intended text, styles, and links before it is accepted; ambiguous structures receive a disclosed literal-text fallback with safe URLs. Arbitrary document CSS is not applied. An opening H1 becomes the title field. Heading line breaks become spaces with a notice. Imported thematic breaks become *** in Markdown. Plain Markdown also supports ordinary paragraphs underlined with = (H1) or - (H2); an opening underlined H1 follows the same title-field rules. A first line of --- in a Markdown file still starts front matter, so use *** for an opening break. The converted article supports up to 60,000 characters; escaping literal punctuation can increase its size. Lists support six levels and 200 items; tables support twelve columns and 100 data rows.</p>
<h3>Review changes before applying</h3><p>Fonts, colors, page layout, and unsupported inline formatting are not preserved. Images become text placeholders with any supplied alternative description. Embedded and interactive content and explicitly hidden content are omitted. Unsafe or relative link targets are removed while labels remain. Multiline emphasis, overly complex inline formatting, and paragraph breaks within list items or cells may be simplified, with notices. Merged or nested tables, tracked changes, reversed or lettered lists, unusual nesting, and overly complex or oversized HTML are rejected before replacing your article. Resolve those structures in your source or import a smaller selection.</p>
<p>HTML is parsed locally in detached template contents. Original source nodes are never attached to the page. The reading preview is built from converted Markdown, not the original HTML. No source images or embedded resources are loaded, and no upload or AI request is made. Original HTML, choices, and pending previews are temporary and excluded from backups. After applying, only the converted article fields become workspace writing. This is article preparation, not a round-trip document editor; inspect your destination before publishing.</p>

<h3>Open a Word .docx directly</h3><p>For a file-first starting point, use the <a href="/word-to-wordpress/">Word to WordPress converter</a>. It uses the same local preview, checkpoint protection, and exports. Save current writing before changing tools.</p><ol><li>Select <strong>Import formatted writing</strong>, then <strong>Choose a Word document or article file</strong>. Select a plain .docx up to 8 MiB. No file is uploaded.</li><li>Inspect the automatically staged reading preview, Markdown, page details, and notices. Ordinary paragraphs, heading styles, bold and italic runs, ordinary single strikethrough, safe web links, bullets, consecutive decimal lists, and simple header tables are supported.</li><li>If a table has no marked header, choose the first-row header option only when its first row contains column names, then preview the selected file again.</li><li>If you want embedded PNG or JPEG files, choose <strong>Prepare Word image files</strong> in the preview. Inspect recovered files and omissions, acknowledge the original-file contents, and choose <strong>Download images &amp; placement notes</strong> before importing. Use the ZIP instructions to upload images and replace numbered placeholders in WordPress.</li><li>Acknowledge the conversion, then import. Cancelling replacement keeps your current writing and approval. Applying checkpoints valid previous writing, keeps source notes, replaces article fields and review notes, and clears approval.</li></ol><p>The main document and supported referenced footnotes and endnotes are converted. Images and embedded objects become visible text placeholders. An empty ordinary paragraph with a single bottom border becomes a thematic break with a notice; other paragraph borders are omitted with a notice. Borders inside table cells do not become separators. Headers, footers, document metadata, fonts, colors, and page layout are not imported. Comments stay out of article text; the separate comment recovery action can keep feedback privately. Explicitly hidden text is omitted. Keep your original file and compare the conversion with it. Tracked changes are blocked by default. Ordinary inline text insertions and deletions can use an explicit proposed/previous version preview after file selection; see <a href="#word-text-revisions">Word text revisions</a>. Complex revisions, fields (including automatic tables of contents), equations, merged or nested tables, lettered/custom numbering, and unsupported structures are rejected. Resolve or simplify those in Word first, or import a smaller formatted selection. Old .doc, macro-enabled, encrypted, ZIP64, damaged, and unusually packaged files are not supported.</p><p>Word files use a bounded local ZIP reader: at most 256 entries, 32 MiB of declared expanded content, 2 MiB per selected XML part, and 4 MiB of selected XML in total. Ordinary conversion decompresses up to ten allowlisted XML parts, including footnote/endnote content and their relationships, with size and checksum checks. Explicit image preparation separately reads supported embedded PNG and JPEG parts. It covers the first 200 visible image or object positions and up to 40 files, each at most 4 MiB and together at most 12 MiB. Original image bytes, Word alternative text, converted article text, and placement notes go into the separate ZIP; linked images are not fetched and embedded objects are not recovered. Original files can include metadata and areas hidden by cropping. Images are not displayed here, and Word crops, rotation, effects, and layout are not reproduced. Applying or clearing the import discards the prepared package; it is excluded from workspace backups and ordinary article exports. See <a href="/word-to-wordpress/#keep-word-images">Word image recovery steps</a>. XML is parsed in detached documents, then converted to the same bounded Markdown format used by the HTML importer. Modern browser support for raw DEFLATE decompression is required for compressed files; if unavailable, use a formatted selection. The converted article still has a 60,000-character limit. Selected bytes, previews, and import choices are temporary, excluded from backups, and cleared on apply or clearing the import.</p>
<!--WORD_NOTES_HELP-->
<h2 id="copied-writing">Paste a copied Google Docs selection</h2><p><a href="/google-docs-to-wordpress/">Open the Google Docs to WordPress tool</a> for a paste-first workspace. In formatted-selection mode, a user-initiated paste previews immediately and the box empties for the next selection. HTML-only clipboard data is supported; plain-only data keeps punctuation literally, receives a warning and needs acknowledgement. Headings, Markdown formatting, page details, and outer reply fences are not interpreted in that fallback. Choose Markdown in Import format to interpret intentional Markdown. Literal escaping counts toward the 60,000-character article limit; oversized conversions are rejected in full. The original copied source stays in tab memory for re-preview after the header choice changes. Oversized or empty clipboard data keeps the earlier preview. A conversion failure retains bounded source for retry and blocks applying an earlier visible preview until a selection converts successfully. Typing new source, changing formats, clearing, full workspace replacement, or applying discards it. Clipboard source is excluded from backups and article exports. No Google account connection, background clipboard read, permission prompt, or upload occurs. Ordinary pasting in the article body remains plain text.</p>

<p>Ordinary single-line Word strikethrough and HTML <code>s</code>, <code>strike</code>, or inline CSS <code>line-through</code> can become double-tilde article Markdown. It stays struck through in supported previews and WordPress, HTML, and Word exports. Matching tildes must form runs of exactly two; single tildes, runs of three or more, multiline or excessive inline structures may remain literal. Double strikethrough is simplified with a warning. Level-one imported headings become plain title text with a warning when struck; title fields do not retain formatting. Imported blockquotes already simplify to quoted text. HTML <code>ins</code> and <code>del</code> remain blocked as possible tracked changes. This does not accept revisions automatically. Plain clipboard punctuation stays literal.</p>
